  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Saturday)\nIssued at 219 PM CDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nUpper-level ridging will continue to build across west Texas and\nsoutheast New Mexico Tuesday through Thursday. A southerly to \nsoutheasterly surface flow will also prevail each day as surface \nhigh pressure shifts over the eastern CONUS and as a surface \ntrough takes shape in the lee of the Rockies. Mostly clear skies\nand dry weather conditions will continue through Thursday\nafternoon. High temperatures will trend back to near to slightly\nabove normal values in the upper 70s to mid 80s for most places,\nwhile overnight lows generally range in the mid 50s to lower 60s.\nThe next system we will be monitoring will be an upper-level\ntrough lifting over the Desert Southwest Thursday night into\nFriday. The upper-level ridge will shift to the east of our region\nlate this week as southwesterly flow aloft increases in advance of\nthis system. Increasing moisture/ascent will bring a chance of\nrain showers and a few thunderstorms back into the forecast over\nwestern portions of our forecast area Thursday night and Friday.\nAdditional tropical moisture may spread northward over Mexico into\nwest Texas and southeast Mexico Friday night and Saturday in the\nsouthwesterly flow aloft ahead of the trough. Confidence remains\nlow on the progression of the trough late this week into this\nweekend, so for now rain chances remain highest over western\nportions of the CWA (generally 30-50%) Friday night into Saturday,\nand lower (10-30%) over our eastern zones during this time. Little\nchange in daily temperatures is forecast through next Saturday,\nwith highs generally averaging in the 70s and 80s with morning\nlows in the 50s to lower 60s.",
